solution:
Rearranging $x^2 - x - 1= 0$ gives $x^2 = x + 1$.  So, repeatedly substituting $x+1$ for $x^2$ gives us \begin{align*}
x^3 - 2x + 1 &= x(x^2)-2x + 1\\
&=x(x+1) - 2x + 1\\
&= x^2 + x -2x + 1\\
&= x^2 - x + 1\\
&= (x+1) - x + 1\\
&=\boxed{2}
\end{align*}

The common ratio between consecutive terms is $\frac{3}{5}$ (you can choose any two consecutive terms and divide the second one by the first to find the common ratio). So the $n^\text{th}$ term of the sequence is $\frac{125}{9} \cdot \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^{n-1}$. Plugging in $n=8$, we get $$
\frac{125}{9} \cdot \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^{7} = \frac{5^3}{3^2} \cdot \frac{3^7}{5^7}
= \frac{3^5}{5^4}
= \boxed{\frac{243}{625}}.
$$

We have the equation $\frac{2x-1}{2x+2}=\frac{x-3}{x-1}$. Cross-multiplying and simplifying, we get \begin{align*}
(2x-1)(x-1)&=(2x+2)(x-3)\\
2x^2 - x - 2x + 1 &= 2x^2 + 2x - 3 \cdot 2x - 3 \cdot 2 \\
2x^2 - 3x + 1&=2x^2-4x-6\\
x&=\boxed{-7}
\end{align*}

We can rephrase the problem as the system of equations: \begin{align*}
\frac{a+2b}{2} &= 7\\
\frac{a+2c}{2} &= 8
\end{align*} Adding these gives: \begin{align*}
\frac{a+2b}{2}+\frac{a+2c}{2} &= 7+8\\
\frac{2a+2b+2c}{2} &= 15\\
a+b+c &= 15\\
\frac{a+b+c}{3} &= \frac{15}{3} = \boxed{5}
\end{align*}

